  "reasoning": "The patient presents with burning epigastric pain, gas, and bloating, pointing to upper gastrointestinal distress. Weight loss and loss of appetite suggest a chronic condition impacting digestion or causing food aversion due to pain. The presence of dark tarry stools (melena) and blood in vomit (hematemesis) are definitive signs of upper gastrointestinal bleeding. Together, these symptoms form a classic clinical picture of peptic ulcer disease, where mucosal erosions in the stomach or duodenum cause pain and can erode into blood vessels leading to hemorrhage. This aligns precisely with the reference cases describing stomach pain, bloating, and related digestive complications. The diagnosis is rendered as a single unit matching the reference vocabulary and answer shape.",
